R e a d m e
-----------

PathfinderPatrol orchestrates a simple two-point patrol loop by driving the Pathfinder
navigation script. Drop this programmable block script on the same grid as:

- a functional `IMyRemoteControl`
- at least one programmable block running the `PathfindingNavigation` script (name it with `[PF]`)
- the Pathfinder mod (for the hidden terminal properties)

Initial Setup
-------------
1. Load this script into a programmable block (PB). Give the block a clear name; the script will pass
   that name to Pathfinder when it asks it to run.
2. Place the Pathfinder navigation PB on the same grid and include `[PF]` in its Custom Name so the patrol
   script can locate it automatically.
3. (Optional) Tag any LCD/Panel you want to use for status output with `[PP]`. The PB’s surface is used
   automatically.
4. Run the script once. It populates the PB’s Custom Data with:

```
=== PathfinderPatrol Start ===
PointA: PointA:0.00:0.00:0.00
PointB: PointB:0.00:0.00:0.00
WaitDuration: 5
StartOnLoad: False
=== PathfinderPatrol End ===
```

5. Replace `PointA` and `PointB` with GPS coordinates (`GPS:Name:X:Y:Z:` or `Name:X:Y:Z`). Adjust
   `WaitDuration` (seconds to pause at each waypoint) and set `StartOnLoad` to `True` if the patrol should
   resume automatically after a world load.

How It Works
------------
- `PathfinderPatrol` alternates between Point A and Point B. When it requests Pathfinder to travel to the
  next waypoint it issues `start <GPS> <PatrolPBName>` to the `[PF]` programmable block.
- PathfinderNavigation, when properly configured, calls back into this script with `pf_start`, `pf_stop`,
  and `pf_nopath` commands so the patrol can advance, wait, or abort. No timers are required.
- Status text showing the current state plus any detail messages is written to every `[PP]` panel and to
  the patrol PB’s LCD.

Commands
--------
Run these in the patrol PB’s terminal:

- `start` – begin the patrol loop (or resume if it was paused)
- `stop` – halt immediately and cancel any pending wait
- `pf_start` / `pf_stop` / `pf_nopath` – normally triggered by PathfinderNavigation; exposed in case you
  need to test the callbacks manually

Tips & Troubleshooting
----------------------
- The patrol will not start unless both PointA and PointB are valid GPS coordinates.
- If the script prints “Pathfinder… not found”, check that the Pathfinder PB’s name contains `[PF]`.
- When running multiple patrol craft, make sure each patrol PB has a unique name; that name is how
  Pathfinder knows which PB to call back.
- To monitor path progress, enable “StartOnLoad” only after verifying the patrol runs cleanly once.

Stay groovy and happy patrolling.

